# Transport Modes

Choosing the right transport depends on your network environment and operational goals.

## Choosing a Transport

If UDP is blocked or rate-limited — common in corporate networks and cloud environments — use **TCP (WebSocket)**. It appears as standard HTTPS traffic and can pass through most HTTP proxies. 

On reliable networks where both are available, **WebSocket** is the recommended default. It offers lower latency and equivalent throughput due to its well-optimized path through the operating system.

Use **UDP (QUIC)** when packet loss is above ~1%. QUIC's per-stream loss recovery prevents a single dropped packet from stalling the entire tunnel, making it more resilient for multiple concurrent connections.

## UDP (QUIC)

The default transport. Native stream multiplexing and built-in TLS 1.3. Append `/udp` to the address, or omit the suffix entirely.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/udp" /%}

## TCP (WebSockets)

Traffic is wrapped in WebSockets to appear as standard HTTPS to proxies, firewalls, and Deep Packet Inspection (DPI) tools. Append `/tcp` to the address.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/tcp" /%}

### CDN Fronting

With TLS enabled, WebSocket mode can be fronted through a CDN, making tunnel traffic appear as legitimate HTTPS to a known CDN domain. This adds an additional layer of obfuscation and makes the connection significantly harder to attribute or block.

{% codeblock code="wallhack entry --listen :443/tcp --cert cert.pem --key key.pem" /%}
